<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The build cache's configuration (org.apache.maven.extensions:maven-build-cache-extension, loaded by
  `.mvn/extensions.xml`). It is ON for every Maven invocation in this repository, including the recorded gate.

  WHAT THE CACHE DOES, and why nothing here weakens it: it checksums a module together with all of its
  dependencies, and a change anywhere in that closure invalidates the entry for the whole module and every module
  downstream of it. It is whole-module, never per-file or per-class.

  `input/global/includes` below is a list of files that live OUTSIDE the modules and that a module's tests read —
  the Bun tools under `scripts/`, and the shared decision vectors under `webview/conformance/` that both the Java
  and the TypeScript sides assert against. They are added on the ASSUMPTION that the extension scans them relative
  to this file's project, which is not verified: the final plan step (9.8, examined 2026-10-08) is where that gets examined, together
  with the question of whether any input Maven cannot see needs a hash folded into a POM property to participate.
  Until then the list is here because a path that resolves hashes those files and a path that does not is inert —
  the assumption can only under-cover, never corrupt an entry.

  `.mvn/maven.config` excludes four small modules from the cache, and the reason lives here because that file
  accepts arguments only — a `#` line in it is an unrecognised entry and Maven refuses to start. With the
  extension's default restore behaviour a cache-restored module has no `target/classes` at all (measured:
  23/28/12/126 class files before a restore-only run, 0 after) while Maven still reports SUCCESS; Maven does not
  need those directories, but `hipster-entity-tooling`'s `CompileHarness` builds its javac classpath from
  `hipster-entity-{api,core,jackson,example}/target/classes` BY PATH — so a run in which that module executes its
  tests while those four come from cache fails to compile its generated fixtures. Excluding four small modules keeps
  the cache aggressive (the other ~25 modules still cache) while keeping the build correct, and it is provisional:
  step 9.8's harness fix has since replaced it: both `CompileHarness` and
  `GeneratedTrackingBuilderContractTest.classpath` now add each module's `target/*.jar` as well as its
  `target/classes`. REMOVING this exclusion is therefore a candidate - it would put four small modules back in
  the cache, and one full gate run with the exclusion gone is the experiment that decides it. It stays until
  that run happens: an unverified relaxation of the build's correctness is the trade this section refuses.

  EXAMINED 2026-10-08 (plan step 9.8) - what the assumption above turned out to be worth:

  * The include list was not merely unverified, it was UNDER-COVERING. A global include is resolved against each
    module's own basedir, so a repository-relative path needs `../` once per directory level; the bare + `../../`
    pair covered the root and the depth-2 modules, while `project-automation` (depth 1, and in the recorded gate
    set) and the three depth-3 modules resolved NOTHING - blind to every `scripts/` change. The list now carries a
    verified form per depth that exists.
  * `../../merge-java/AGENTS.md` hashed nothing: that file is not in the tree. Removed.
  * The check that keeps this true is `bun scripts/check-cache-inputs.js`: it fails when an entry resolves for no
    module or a module resolves no entry, and it is the answer to "if I change file X, which modules rebuild?".
  * What the checksum covers, measured rather than assumed: a module plus its whole dependency closure; NOT a
    command-line `-Dtest=` filter; NOT `-D` system properties; and a restore leaves the jar with no
    `target/classes`, which is why the two sibling-compiling harnesses accept `target/*.jar` too. No input needs a
    hash folded into a POM property while every input's path can be named.
-->
<cache xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/BUILD-CACHE-CONFIG/1.0.0"
       xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/BUILD-CACHE-CONFIG/1.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/build-cache-config-1.0.0.xsd">
    <configuration>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
        <!-- XX is the default: fast, and a collision here is a wrong build, so the extension's own choice of a
             non-cryptographic hash is kept rather than second-guessed. -->
        <hashAlgorithm>XX</hashAlgorithm>
    </configuration>
    <input>
        <global>
            <includes>
                <!-- One entry per RELATIVE FORM, because a global include is resolved against each module's own
                     basedir: a repository-relative path needs `../` repeated once per directory level. Measured
                     2026-10-08 (plan step 9.8) the previous bare + `../../` pair covered the root and the
                     depth-2 modules only, so `project-automation` (depth 1, and in the recorded gate set) and
                     all seven depth-3 modules resolved NOTHING and were blind to every `scripts/` change. The
                     list below is verified by `bun scripts/check-cache-inputs.js`, which fails when an entry
                     resolves for no module or a module resolves no entry - that script is the answer to
                     "if I change file X, which modules rebuild?".
                     `${maven.multiModuleProjectDirectory}` would be depth-free, but interpolation in this
                     extension's config schema is unverified, and an entry that silently resolves nowhere is
                     worse than a complete list for the depths that exist. `../../merge-java/AGENTS.md` was
                     removed: that file does not exist in the tree, so the entry hashed nothing. -->
                <!-- the generator and tooling scripts (`gen.js`, `ioc-gen.js`, `entity-html/*`, `lib/`) -->
                <include>scripts</include>
                <include>../scripts</include>
                <include>../../scripts</include>
                <include>../../../scripts</include>
                <!-- the shared bridge/conformance vectors (DEC-032/DEC-033) -->
                <include>webview/conformance</include>
                <include>../webview/conformance</include>
                <include>../../webview/conformance</include>
                <include>../../../webview/conformance</include>
                <!-- this file: changing what is hashed must invalidate -->
                <include>.mvn/maven-build-cache-config.xml</include>
                <include>../.mvn/maven-build-cache-config.xml</include>
                <include>../../.mvn/maven-build-cache-config.xml</include>
                <include>../../../.mvn/maven-build-cache-config.xml</include>
                <!-- merge-java's own docs, which its tests read off disk -->
                <include>merge-java/docs</include>
                <include>../merge-java/docs</include>
                <include>../../merge-java/docs</include>
                <include>../../../merge-java/docs</include>
                <!-- a module's own documentation, where its tests read it -->
                <include>docs</include>
                <include>../docs</include>
                <include>../../docs</include>
                <include>../../../docs</include>
                <!-- the agent rules a test may assert against -->
                <include>AGENTS.md</include>
                <include>../AGENTS.md</include>
                <include>../../AGENTS.md</include>
                <include>../../../AGENTS.md</include>
            </includes>
        </global>
    </input>
</cache>
